{% set design %}
<section class="docs-section">
  <h2 id="d-usage">Usage</h2>
  <p>The rail shell is the middle ground between the bottom-nav shell and the
     drawer: on phones it is a standard bottom tab bar, and from 905px up the
     same element becomes a permanent 80px icon rail on the left edge.</p>
  <h3>Do use it when</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>You have 3–5 destinations that deserve <strong>permanent visibility on
        desktop</strong>, but a 280px drawer column is too much.</li>
    <li>Your destinations are icon-shaped (Photos, Files, Music) — the rail is
        icon-only on desktop.</li>
    <li>You want one nav element for both layouts — it literally reuses the
        bottom-nav markup.</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Don't use it when</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Destinations need visible text labels on desktop — the rail is icon-only
        there; use the drawer shell instead.</li>
    <li>You have more than five destinations — nothing fits an 80px rail.</li>
    <li>Desktop users need secondary nav sections or an account footer — that is
        drawer territory.</li>
  </ul>
  {{ callout({
    variant: "info",
    title: "Compared to the other shells",
    text: "Bottom-nav: same bar on both breakpoints. Rail: bar → icon rail. Drawer: hidden column → permanent 280px nav.",
    href: "drawer.html",
    linkText: "Drawer shell"
  }) }}
</section>

