<section class="docs-section">
  <h2 id="d-behavior">Behavior</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>&lt;905px:</strong> identical to the bottom-nav shell — fixed bottom bar, stacked icon-over-label tabs.</li>
    <li><strong>≥905px:</strong> the bar moves into a left grid column: 80px wide, vertical, top-aligned, 1px right border, no top border.</li>
    <li><strong>No pills on desktop:</strong> unlike the bottom-nav's desktop bar, rail tabs opt out of the tinted pill — selection is accent color only, matching the M2 navigation-rail spec.</li>
    <li><strong>Selection:</strong> <code>aria-current="page"</code>, reflected by <code>flint.js</code> inside <code>[data-fl-nav]</code>; works identically at both breakpoints.</li>
    <li><strong>Badges:</strong> absolute over the icon on desktop (there is no room beside it), inline everywhere else.</li>
    <li>The rail is always visible on desktop — there is no collapsed/expanded state and no JS required for the layout switch.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section class="docs-section">
  <h2 id="d-accessibility">Accessibility</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The nav element carries <code>aria-label="Primary"</code>; it stays a single landmark at both breakpoints, so screen readers never see two navs.</li>
    <li>Exactly one item carries <code>aria-current="page"</code>.</li>
    <li>Rail items are buttons or links with visible text labels — the 80px rail keeps the label, so nothing relies on <code>title</code> or tooltips.</li>
    <li>Icons are <code>aria-hidden</code>; the label carries the meaning.</li>
    <li>Include <code>viewport-fit=cover</code> in the meta viewport for safe-area support on phones.</li>
  </ul>
</section>
{% endset %}

